<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław III of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1424">1424</a>–<a href="/years/1444">1444</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Vladislaus I of Hungary, Władysław Warneńczyk · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1434 and, as Vladislaus I, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1440 until his death. His election in Hungary against the infant Ladislaus V led to civil war, but with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> he waged the Long Campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1443–1444. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-varna">Battle of Varna</a> in 1444, earning the name Warneńczyk, 'of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland.jpg" alt="Władysław III of Poland" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_Warne%C5%84czyk_seal_1438_(cropped).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Władysław was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 31 October 1424, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and his fourth wife, the Lithuanian noblewoman Sophia of Halshany. His father was elderly, and the birth of a male heir was widely regarded as a miracle. In 1427 Polish nobles of the anti-Jagiellonian opposition tried to have Jogaila's sons declared illegitimate to the throne, since they had no blood relation to the Piast and Anjou kings. He succeeded his father in 1434, shortly before his tenth birthday. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki">Zbigniew Oleśnicki</a> acted as his guardian and a council of provisores governed in his name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> nobles, who had been excluded from the vote, favoured the Piast candidate Siemowit V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>. Władysław was crowned at Wawel Cathedral on 25 July 1434 under a revised ritual that obliged him to swear an oath before his anointing, so that the throne seemed to depend on meeting the nobles' requirements rather than on hereditary right. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> declared him of age at Piotrków in 1438, though he is believed to have had little voice in politics. Spytko III of Melsztyn, who sympathised with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>, rose against Oleśnicki in 1439 and was killed at the Battle of Grotniki; the king returned his body to his widow and restored the family's privileges.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-germany">Albert II of Germany</a> died in 1439, the Hungarian Estates, wary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the Ottoman threat, rejected an infant king, and elected Władysław on 8 March 1440. Albert's widow Elizabeth of Luxembourg backed her infant son Ladislaus the Posthumous, and civil war followed. The east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> supported Władysław and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, while the north and west stood with the queen, who hired Hussite mercenaries under John Jiskra. Her death in 1442 strengthened his position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Urged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the legate Julian Cesarini, Władysław launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna">Crusade of Varna</a>. On 10 November 1444 an Ottoman army of about 60,000 met about 16,000 crusaders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and he was reportedly killed in a cavalry charge against Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>. His body was never recovered, which gave rise to legends of survival, none of them confirmed. He was succeeded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> by his brother Casimir IV and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> by Ladislaus V the Posthumous.</p><p>His record as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was blackened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> polemic, which portrayed him as a usurper and spread the rumour that he had poisoned Elizabeth. He remains a notable figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, where a boulevard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> bears his name and a park-museum with a cenotaph opened in 1935.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Władysław III of Poland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sigismund's crusading army was crushed at the Battle of Nicopolis in 1396, and King <b>Vladislaus I</b> fell at the Battle of Varna in 1444.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the historian Jan Długosz, Polish knights sang it at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410 and before other notable battles in the years that followed; it also accompanied the coronation of <b>Władysław III of Poland</b> and of the first Jagiellonian kings.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Władysław III of Poland</b> and Hungary was killed, and the defeat ended the Crusade of Varna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Varna, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1437" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Władysław III of Poland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1437">What was here in 1437? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
